Use Testcontainers. Spring Boot can take the connection details from the container, so you don't set a URL anywhere. There are two ways to do it.

## 1. `@ServiceConnection` (preferred)

Annotate the container with `@ServiceConnection`. Spring Boot then creates the connection details bean for it. For a `JdbcDatabaseContainer`, such as `PostgreSQLContainer`, that covers `JdbcConnectionDetails`. It also covers Flyway and Liquibase connection details [S2]. R2DBC is supported for `PostgreSQLContainer`, `MySQLContainer` and similar containers [S2].

The docs I retrieved show `@ServiceConnection` only on a `@Bean` method. This is their Redis example:

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class MyRedisConfiguration {

	@Bean
	@ServiceConnection(name = "redis")
	public GenericContainer<?> redisContainer() {
		return new GenericContainer<>("redis:7");
	}

}
```

- **Typed containers:** with a typed container such as `Neo4jContainer`, Spring Boot uses the bean method's return type to pick the connection details [S8]. For a database, return something like `PostgreSQLContainer` the same way.
- **`GenericContainer`:** Spring Boot can't tell which image it is. You must give a hint with `name`, as in `name = "redis"` above [S8].
- **Custom or mirrored images:** `name` also overrides which connection details are used [S8].

## 2. `@DynamicPropertySource` (more manual, more flexible)

Register properties whose values are read from the container after it starts [S6]:

```java
@Testcontainers
@SpringBootTest
class MyIntegrationTests {

	@Container
	static Neo4jContainer neo4j = new Neo4jContainer("neo4j:5");

	@DynamicPropertySource
	static void neo4jProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
		registry.add("spring.neo4j.uri", neo4j::getBoltUrl);
	}
}
```

For a JDBC database, you would register `spring.datasource.url` the same way. The retrieved docs only show the Neo4j example, so the property name there is my extrapolation.

## Slice tests

`@DataJpaTest`, `@JdbcTest` and similar tests replace your database with an in-memory one by default. To use the real container, add `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase`, as described for `@DataJpaTest` [S3].

`@DataJpaTest` comes from the **`spring-boot-data-jpa-test`** module [S6].

**What it sets up** [S6]:
- It scans for `@Entity` classes and configures Spring Data JPA repositories.
- If an embedded database is on the classpath, it configures one.
- It logs SQL queries by setting `spring.jpa.show-sql=true`. You can turn this off with the annotation's `showSql` attribute.
- It does not scan regular `@Component` or `@ConfigurationProperties` beans. Use `@EnableConfigurationProperties` to include `@ConfigurationProperties` beans.
- Tests are transactional and roll back after each test.
- You can inject a `TestEntityManager`, and a `JdbcTemplate` is also available [S1].

The docs point to an appendix for the full list of auto-configurations. That list wasn't in my search results.

**Disable the rollback**:
```java
import org.springframework.boot.data.jpa.test.autoconfigure.DataJpaTest;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Propagation;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@DataJpaTest
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)
class MyNonTransactionalTests { }
```

**Use a real database instead of the embedded one** [S1]:
```java
import org.springframework.boot.data.jpa.test.autoconfigure.DataJpaTest;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace;

@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)
class MyRepositoryTests { }
```

**Package names in Boot 4**: the imports are now module-specific. `DataJpaTest` is in `org.springframework.boot.data.jpa.test.autoconfigure`. `TestEntityManager` is in `org.springframework.boot.jpa.test.autoconfigure` [S1]. `AutoConfigureTestDatabase` is in `org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure` [S1].

To use `TestEntityManager` in a test that isn't `@DataJpaTest`, add `@AutoConfigureTestEntityManager`. That test must also run in a transaction [S1].

Yes. Use `@DynamicPropertySource`. A static method of that kind adds property values to the Spring `Environment`, and the values can come from a running container [S1].

```java
@Testcontainers
@SpringBootTest
class MyIntegrationTests {

	@Container
	static Neo4jContainer neo4j = new Neo4jContainer("neo4j:5");

	@Test
	void myTest() {
		// ...
	}

	@DynamicPropertySource
	static void neo4jProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
		registry.add("spring.neo4j.uri", neo4j::getBoltUrl);
	}

}
```

Each value is registered as a `Supplier`, here `neo4j::getBoltUrl`. Because of that, it's read from the container when it's needed, so the container's mapped host and port are available. With this setup, the application's Neo4j beans connect to the container Testcontainers manages [S1].

The docs describe this as "a slightly more verbose but also more flexible alternative to service connections" [S1]. If your container type has service connection support, that is the less verbose option. The passages I found don't go into how service connections work.

The docs also note a lifecycle caveat. When Testcontainers manages the containers rather than Spring beans, the shutdown order of beans and containers isn't guaranteed. Containers can stop before the beans that depend on them are cleaned up, which can cause exceptions such as lost connections. If the context needs to stay usable while it's cached, the docs suggest managing containers as Spring beans or importing container declarations with `@ImportTestcontainers` [S6].

For setting plain, non-container properties in tests, there's also `TestPropertyValues`. It adds `key=value` pairs to a `ConfigurableEnvironment` or `ConfigurableApplicationContext` [S2].

Annotate a field in the test class with `@MockitoBean`. Spring Framework provides this annotation, and it defines a Mockito mock for a bean in the `ApplicationContext` [S1]. Use `@MockitoSpyBean` if you want a spy instead [S1]. You don't need a configuration class.

This example is a `@WebFluxTest` slice test from the docs [S5]:

```java
import org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito.MockitoBean;

@WebFluxTest(UserVehicleController.class)
class MyControllerTests {

	@Autowired
	private WebTestClient webClient;

	@MockitoBean
	private UserVehicleService userVehicleService;

	@Test
	void testExample() {
		given(this.userVehicleService.getVehicleDetails("sboot"))
			.willReturn(new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic"));
		this.webClient.get().uri("/sboot/vehicle").accept(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN).exchange()
			.expectStatus().isOk()
			.expectBody(String.class).isEqualTo("Honda Civic");
	}
}
```

The annotation lives in `org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito`, not in a Boot package [S5]. The documentation I found doesn't go into details such as how a bean is matched or replaced. The Boot docs point to the Spring Framework documentation for those [S1].

Use `@MockitoSpyBean`. It comes from Spring Framework and wraps a Mockito spy around the bean in your `ApplicationContext` [S1]. The Spring Boot docs I searched only mention it briefly and don't include a spy example. They point to the Spring Framework documentation for details [S1].

The sketch below is from my own knowledge, not from the retrieved passages. It uses the same package as `@MockitoBean`, which appears in the docs' examples as `org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito.MockitoBean` [S2].

```java
@SpringBootTest
class MyTests {

    @MockitoSpyBean
    private MyService myService;   // the real bean, wrapped in a spy

    @Test
    void callsService() {
        // exercise code that uses the bean...
        verify(myService).doSomething();
    }
}
```

For the full semantics, such as spying by type or by name, check the Spring Framework reference. The documentation I can search doesn't cover them.

Use `@WebMvcTest`. It comes from the `spring-boot-webmvc-test` module [S2].

**What it does**
- It auto-configures the Spring MVC infrastructure. It limits scanning to web-layer beans: `@Controller`, `@ControllerAdvice`, `@JacksonComponent`, `Converter`, `Filter`, `HandlerInterceptor`, `WebMvcConfigurer` and similar [S2].
- It doesn't scan regular `@Component` or `@ConfigurationProperties` beans. You can add `@ConfigurationProperties` beans with `@EnableConfigurationProperties` [S2].
- It auto-configures `MockMvc`, so you don't need a full HTTP server. If AssertJ is available, it also auto-configures `MockMvcTester` [S2].
- It's usually limited to one controller and combined with `@MockitoBean` to mock the controller's collaborators [S2].

```java
import org.springframework.boot.webmvc.test.autoconfigure.WebMvcTest;
import org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito.MockitoBean;
import org.springframework.test.web.servlet.assertj.MockMvcTester;

@WebMvcTest(UserVehicleController.class)
class MyControllerTests {

	@Autowired
	private MockMvcTester mvc;

	@MockitoBean
	private UserVehicleService userVehicleService;

	@Test
	void testExample() {
		given(this.userVehicleService.getVehicleDetails("sboot"))
			.willReturn(new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic"));
		assertThat(this.mvc.get().uri("/sboot/vehicle").accept(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN))
			.hasStatusOk()
			.hasBodyTextEqualTo("Honda Civic");
	}
}
```
[S1]

**Alternative:** `@SpringBootTest` with `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` also gives you `MockMvc`. It loads a full web `ApplicationContext` but doesn't start a server [S3, S4]. The docs recommend `@WebMvcTest` if you want to focus only on the web layer and not start a complete `ApplicationContext` [S5].

Use Spring REST Docs through `@AutoConfigureRestDocs`. It comes from the `spring-boot-restdocs` module and replaces the Spring REST Docs JUnit extension [S2].

**Basic usage**

Add `@AutoConfigureRestDocs` to a `@WebMvcTest`. Then call `document("...")` on the result of a request. This is the documentation's example [S3]:

```java
@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)
@AutoConfigureRestDocs
class MyUserDocumentationTests {

	@Autowired
	private MockMvcTester mvc;

	@Test
	void listUsers() {
		assertThat(this.mvc.get().uri("/users").accept(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN)).hasStatusOk()
			.apply(document("list-users"));
	}

}
```

The `document` method is a static import from `MockMvcRestDocumentation` [S3]. The documentation says the `MockMvc` bean is customized to use Spring REST Docs, and that you can inject it and use it as usual [S3]. It also says `MockMvcTester` reuses the same `MockMvc` instance, so any configuration applies to both [S3].

**Output directory and URIs**

Snippets go to `target/generated-snippets` with Maven or `build/generated-snippets` with Gradle by default [S2]. `@AutoConfigureRestDocs` can override that directory. It can also set the host, scheme and port that appear in documented URIs [S2].

**Generating snippets for every call**

To document every `MockMvc` call automatically, define a `RestDocumentationResultHandler` bean. The auto-configuration calls `alwaysDo` with it, so each call generates the default snippets [S1]:

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class MyResultHandlerConfiguration {

	@Bean
	public RestDocumentationResultHandler restDocumentation() {
		return MockMvcRestDocumentation.document("{method-name}");
	}

}
```

**Further configuration**

For more control than the annotation attributes give, implement a `RestDocsMockMvcConfigurationCustomizer` bean. This example switches the snippet format to Markdown [S1]:

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class MyRestDocsConfiguration implements RestDocsMockMvcConfigurationCustomizer {

	@Override
	public void customize(MockMvcRestDocumentationConfigurer configurer) {
		configurer.snippets().withTemplateFormat(TemplateFormats.markdown());
	}

}
```

The documentation I searched also covers `WebTestClient` through a `WebTestClientBuilderCustomizer` [S4]. It doesn't cover how to include the generated snippets in Asciidoctor documents.

Use **`RestTestClient`**. The docs recommend it for tests that call a started server [S1].

Annotate the test class with `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient` and autowire the client. It resolves relative URIs against the running server and has its own API for verifying responses [S1].

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@AutoConfigureRestTestClient
class MyRandomPortRestTestClientTests {

	@Test
	void exampleTest(@Autowired RestTestClient restClient) {
		restClient
			.get().uri("/")
			.exchange()
			.expectStatus().isOk()
			.expectBody(String.class).isEqualTo("Hello World");
	}
}
```

- **Random port:** The docs recommend random ports for a full running server. `@LocalServerPort` injects the port that was picked [S1].
- **AssertJ:** Wrap the response with `RestTestClientResponse.from(spec)`. You can then write `assertThat(response).hasStatusOk().bodyText().isEqualTo("Hello World")` [S6].
- **`WebTestClient`:** If `spring-webflux` is on the classpath, you can use `@AutoConfigureWebTestClient` and autowire a `WebTestClient`. It has a similar fluent API [S6].
- **`TestRestTemplate`:** It is still available through `@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate`. It needs the `spring-boot-restclient` dependency, which enables auto-configuration for `RestClient.Builder`. If your main code uses `RestClient.Builder`, put that dependency on the main classpath and not only the test classpath [S4].

Use `@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)`. Each test run picks an available port at random [S2]. By default `@SpringBootTest` doesn't start a server. It sets up a mock environment instead [S4].

You can inject the port that was actually used with `@LocalServerPort` [S2].

To call the running server, autowire a `RestTestClient` by adding `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient` to the test class. It resolves relative URIs against the running server [S2].

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@AutoConfigureRestTestClient
class MyRandomPortRestTestClientTests {

	@Test
	void exampleTest(@Autowired RestTestClient restClient) {
		restClient
			.get().uri("/")
			.exchange()
			.expectStatus().isOk()
			.expectBody(String.class).isEqualTo("Hello World");
	}

}
```

Other options and notes from the docs:
- **AssertJ:** wrap the response with `RestTestClientResponse.from(spec)` and use `assertThat(response).hasStatusOk()...` [S3].
- **WebFlux:** if `spring-webflux` is on the classpath, use `@AutoConfigureWebTestClient` and autowire a `WebTestClient` [S3].
- **TestRestTemplate:** the `spring-boot-resttestclient` module also provides it [S3].
- **Management server:** `RANDOM_PORT` also starts it on a separate random port if your application configures a different management port [S1].
- **Sliced tests:** you may need `@AutoConfigureWebServer` to auto-configure an embedded web server [S3].

The helper class is `TestRestTemplate`. It's a convenience alternative to Spring's `RestTemplate` for integration tests, and it comes from the `spring-boot-resttestclient` module. You also need a dependency on `spring-boot-restclient`. Adding that dependency enables auto-configuration for `RestClient.Builder`. If your main code uses `RestClient.Builder`, put the dependency on the main classpath rather than only the test classpath [S1].

**Using it against a running server**
- Start the server with `@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT)` or `DEFINED_PORT`. Then add `@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate` and inject the template [S2].
- URLs without a host and port go to the embedded server automatically [S2].
- You can customize it through a `RestTemplateBuilder` bean [S2].

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate
class MyTests {
    @Test
    void exampleTest(@Autowired TestRestTemplate restTemplate) {
        String body = restTemplate.getForObject("/", String.class);
        assertThat(body).isEqualTo("Hello World");
    }
}
```

**Security and error handling**
- You can get a vanilla template, or one that sends Basic HTTP authentication with a username and password [S1].
- The template is fault tolerant. It doesn't throw exceptions on 4xx and 5xx responses. You check the status code on the returned `ResponseEntity` instead [S1].

**Redirects**
- The passages I found don't say how redirects are handled.
- They do recommend Apache HttpClient 5.1 or later. If it's on the classpath, `TestRestTemplate` configures the client appropriately [S1].

**Alternatives**
- `RestTestClient` is available with `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient`. It resolves relative links to the running server and has a fluent API for verifying responses [S5].
- With WebFlux, `WebTestClient` offers a similar API [S1].

Use a nested `@TestConfiguration` class in your test. Unlike a nested `@Configuration` class, it doesn't replace your application's primary configuration. Spring Boot loads it in addition to that configuration [S3].

A plain nested `@Configuration` class would replace the primary configuration. Without one, Spring Boot's `@*Test` annotations search upward from the test's package for a `@SpringBootApplication` or `@SpringBootConfiguration` class and use it [S2].

An example (my own sketch, not taken from the docs):

```java
@SpringBootTest
class MyTests {

    @TestConfiguration
    static class Extra {
        @Bean
        Clock fixedClock() {
            return Clock.fixed(Instant.EPOCH, ZoneOffset.UTC);
        }
    }
}
```

- **Sharing across tests:** The search results only describe the nested form. For a shared configuration, the docs say you can use `@Import` on the test to bring in additional configuration classes. That tip appears in the `@WebMvcTest` section, where it's used to register extras such as a `JacksonModule` [S4]. I didn't find a passage that covers `@Import` with `@TestConfiguration` specifically.
- **Slice tests:** `@WebMvcTest` and `@JdbcTest` don't scan regular `@Component` and `@ConfigurationProperties` beans. Use `@Import` or `@EnableConfigurationProperties` to add what you need [S1, S4].
- **Context caching:** Spring caches application contexts between tests. Tests that share the same configuration load the context only once [S3]. Adding a different `@TestConfiguration` to a test gives it a different configuration, which probably means a separate context. That inference is mine, not something the docs state.

**`@JdbcTest`** is the annotation you want. It comes from the `spring-boot-jdbc-test` module. It's meant for tests that only need a `DataSource` and don't use Spring Data JDBC [S2].

- **What it configures:** By default it sets up an in-memory embedded database and a `JdbcTemplate` [S2].
- **What it doesn't scan:** Regular `@Component` and `@ConfigurationProperties` beans aren't scanned. You can use `@EnableConfigurationProperties` to include `@ConfigurationProperties` beans [S2].
- **Transactions:** Tests are transactional and roll back after each test by default. To turn that off, use `@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)` [S2].
- **Real database:** To run against a real database instead of the embedded one, add `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase`, as you would with `@DataJpaTest` [S2].

```java
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.JdbcTest;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Propagation;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@JdbcTest
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)
class MyTransactionalTests {

}
```

If you use Spring Data JDBC repositories, use `@DataJdbcTest` instead. It also configures those repositories [S1].

Use `@JsonTest`. It auto-configures only the JSON mapper, so you don't load the full application context [S3].

**What it configures:** the available JSON library, which can be Jackson, Gson, or Jsonb. For Jackson that means the `JsonMapper`, any `@JacksonComponent` beans, and any Jackson `JacksonModule` [S3]. Jackson 2's `ObjectMapper` is also supported but deprecated [S3].

**Helpers you can `@Autowire`:** `JacksonTester`, `GsonTester`, `JsonbTester`, and `BasicJsonTester` (for Strings) [S3]. They are AssertJ-based and use JSONAssert and JsonPath [S3].

```java
@JsonTest
class MyJsonTests {

	@Autowired
	private JacksonTester<VehicleDetails> json;

	@Test
	void serialize() throws Exception {
		VehicleDetails details = new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic");
		// Assert against a `.json` file in the same package as the test
		assertThat(this.json.write(details)).isEqualToJson("expected.json");
		// Or use JSON path based assertions
		assertThat(this.json.write(details)).hasJsonPathStringValue("@.make");
		assertThat(this.json.write(details)).extractingJsonPathStringValue("@.make").isEqualTo("Honda");
	}

	@Test
	void deserialize() throws Exception {
		String content = "{\"make\":\"Ford\",\"model\":\"Focus\"}";
		assertThat(this.json.parse(content)).isEqualTo(new VehicleDetails("Ford", "Focus"));
		assertThat(this.json.parseObject(content).getMake()).isEqualTo("Ford");
	}

}
```
[S2]

**Notes:**
- To customize the auto-configuration, use `@AutoConfigureJsonTesters` [S3].
- You can use the helpers in plain unit tests without `@JsonTest`. In that case, call the helper's `initFields` method in a `@BeforeEach` method [S2].
- When you assert on a number at a JSON path, `isEqualTo` may not work, depending on the type. Use AssertJ's `satisfies` instead [S2]:

```java
assertThat(this.json.write(value)).extractingJsonPathNumberValue("@.test.numberValue")
	.satisfies((number) -> assertThat(number.floatValue()).isCloseTo(0.15f, within(0.01f)));
```
[S1]

Add `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)` to the test class [S6]:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.data.jpa.test.autoconfigure.DataJpaTest;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace;

@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)
class MyRepositoryTests {

	// ...

}
```

By default, `@DataJpaTest` configures an embedded database if one is on the classpath [S8]. With `Replace.NONE`, that replacement doesn't happen, so the test uses the `DataSource` from your normal configuration. The documentation I searched doesn't say how to supply the real database's connection details. Normally you'd do that with your usual `spring.datasource.*` properties, but that part is my assumption, not something the passages state.

The same annotation works the same way for `@JdbcTest`, `@DataJdbcTest` and `@DataR2dbcTest` [S1, S3, S7].

Use Spring Boot's `OutputCaptureExtension`. It's a JUnit extension that captures `System.out` and `System.err` [S1].

Add `@ExtendWith(OutputCaptureExtension.class)`. Then inject `CapturedOutput` into a test method or into the test class constructor [S1].

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtendWith;

import org.springframework.boot.test.system.CapturedOutput;
import org.springframework.boot.test.system.OutputCaptureExtension;

import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;

@ExtendWith(OutputCaptureExtension.class)
class MyOutputCaptureTests {

	@Test
	void testName(CapturedOutput output) {
		System.out.println("Hello World!");
		assertThat(output).contains("World");
	}

}
```

In the example, AssertJ's `assertThat(output)` asserts directly on the captured output [S1].

Yes. Declare each container as a `@Bean` in a test configuration class, and Spring Boot will manage it for you [S2]:

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
class MyTestConfiguration {

    @Bean
    MongoDBContainer mongoDbContainer() {
        return new MongoDBContainer(DockerImageName.parse("mongo:5.0"));
    }
}
```

Import the configuration into any test that needs it, and autowire the container if you need to access it directly [S2]:

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Import(MyTestConfiguration.class)
class MyIntegrationTests {

    @Autowired
    private MongoDBContainer mongo;
}
```

**Service connections:** The docs say this approach is often combined with service connection annotations [S2]. That means you can add `@ServiceConnection` to the `@Bean` method instead of wiring properties yourself.
- For typed containers such as `MongoDBContainer`, Spring Boot uses the bean method's return type to pick the connection details [S6].
- For a `GenericContainer`, it can't tell which image you use. You then have to give a hint with `@ServiceConnection(name = "redis")` [S6].

**Lifecycle benefits:**
- Container beans are created and started before all other beans. They are stopped after all other beans are destroyed [S3]. Beans that depend on the container can therefore clean up while it is still available.
- A container bean is started once per application context. It is stopped when the TestContext Framework shuts that context down [S1].
- One container instance is often reused across tests from multiple test classes that share a cached context [S1].
- Containers managed by Testcontainers annotations and extensions have no guaranteed shutdown order relative to beans. A container can stop before the beans that use it, which can cause exceptions such as lost connections [S1].
- The docs recommend Spring beans, or `@ImportTestcontainers`, when the context needs to stay usable for as long as it is cached [S1].

The passages I found don't go into `@ImportTestcontainers` usage, so I can't give details on it.

Spring Boot 4.1 has a Redis service connection. Annotate a static Redis container field with `@ServiceConnection`, and Boot creates a `DataRedisConnectionDetails` bean from it. That bean points Data Redis at the container, so you don't set `spring.data.redis.host` or `port` yourself.

The passages I found don't include a Redis-specific example. The code below combines the documented JUnit-extension pattern with the Redis matching rule. Treat it as a sketch, and check the `@ServiceConnection` import and the container class against your Testcontainers version.

```java
@DataRedisTest
@Testcontainers
class MyDataRedisTests {

    @Container
    @ServiceConnection
    static RedisContainer redis = new RedisContainer("redis:7");   // or a GenericContainer named "redis"

    @Autowired
    private SomeRepository repository;

    // tests...
}
```

- **Which containers match:** `DataRedisConnectionDetails` is created for containers of type `RedisContainer` or `RedisStackContainer`. It is also created for containers named "redis", "redis/redis-stack" or "redis/redis-stack-server" [S1].
- **Keep the field static:** By default Boot matches on the image repository name, ignoring the registry and version. That only works when Boot can get the `Container` instance, as with a `static` field [S1].
- **JUnit extension:** `@Testcontainers` starts containers declared with `@Container` on static fields before any test runs. Testcontainers manages their lifecycle [S6].
- **Why `@DataRedisTest`:** It comes from `spring-boot-data-redis-test`, in `org.springframework.boot.data.redis.test.autoconfigure`. It scans `@RedisHash` classes and configures Spring Data Redis repositories [S2].
- **Without a service connection:** The default is `localhost:6379`. You would then have to set `spring.data.redis.*` properties yourself [S5].
- **Lifecycle caveat:** Containers managed by Testcontainers, rather than as Spring beans, give no guarantee about shutdown order. If you want the context to stay usable while it's cached, the docs suggest managing containers as Spring beans or importing them with `@ImportTestcontainers` [S7].

You also need the `spring-boot-testcontainers` dependency, since that jar provides the service connection factories [S3].

Use **`@RestClientTest`**. It comes from the `spring-boot-restclient-test` module, and the import is `org.springframework.boot.restclient.test.autoconfigure.RestClientTest`. [S1]

By default it does the following [S1]:
- It auto-configures Jackson, GSON, and Jsonb support.
- It configures a `RestTemplateBuilder` and a `RestClient.Builder`.
- It adds support for `MockRestServiceServer`.
- It doesn't scan regular `@Component` or `@ConfigurationProperties` beans. Name the beans under test in the `value` or `components` attribute. Use `@EnableConfigurationProperties` if you need `@ConfigurationProperties` beans.

Example with `RestClient.Builder`, where you use the full URI in expectations [S2]:

```java
