**Dependencies**
- `spring-boot-resttestclient` provides `TestRestTemplate` [S1].
- `spring-boot-restclient` is also required [S1, S3].
- The Apache HTTP Client (version 5.1 or better) is recommended but not mandatory. If it's on the classpath, `TestRestTemplate` configures it appropriately [S1].

I didn't find exact Maven or Gradle coordinates in the passages, so the artifact IDs above are the module names the docs use. Check the group ID and scope against your build.

**Side effect on production code**
Adding `spring-boot-restclient` enables auto-configuration for `RestClient.Builder` [S1, S3]. If your main code uses `RestClient.Builder`, declare `spring-boot-restclient` on the main classpath, not only the test classpath [S1, S3]. Otherwise the builder is auto-configured only when tests run, so tests and production would behave differently.

**Usage**
With `@SpringBootTest` using `RANDOM_PORT` or `DEFINED_PORT`, add `@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate` to inject a configured `TestRestTemplate` [S2]:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.resttestclient.TestRestTemplate;
import org.springframework.boot.resttestclient.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate;

@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate
class MyRandomPortTestRestTemplateTests {

	@Test
	void exampleTest(@Autowired TestRestTemplate restTemplate) {
		String body = restTemplate.getForObject("/", String.class);
		assertThat(body).isEqualTo("Hello World");
	}
}
```

You can also instantiate it directly with `new TestRestTemplate()` [S2]. It doesn't throw exceptions on 4xx and 5xx responses, so check the status code on the returned `ResponseEntity` [S1].

The docs also suggest `RestTestClient` if you want a fluent assertion API. For WebFlux, they suggest `WebTestClient` [S1].

